            <ArticleTitle>Value of body mass index in the diagnosis of obesity according to DEXA in well-controlled RA patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>17-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1699-258X(16)00033-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.reuma.2016.02.003</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) has an indirect effect on body composition. Body mass index (BMI) is not a valid predictor of body fat in RA patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the accuracy of BMI in identifying obesity diagnosed according to dual energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) in well-controlled RA patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">An observational, cross-sectional, descriptive, analytical study. We used 3 different cutoffs for obesity as determined by DXA: &gt;35% total fat, &gt;40% total fat, and &gt;35% central fat mass (central obesity).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">One hundred one patients were included. We found that 35% total fat corresponded to a BMI of 24kg/m(2), with a sensitivity of 90% and specificity of 75% (area under the curve [AUC] 0.917); 40% total fat to a BMI of 25kg/m(2), with a sensitivity of 86% and specificity of 39% (AUC 0.822); and 35% central fat mass to a BMI of 22kg/m(2), with a sensitivity of 97% and specificity of 84% (AUC 0.951).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Obesity according to DXA was underdiagnosed when the classic BMI cutoffs were used in well-controlled RA patients.</AbstractText>
